    "summary": "Texas resistance to data centers is strongest where local bodies have already acted: Austin County (countywide moratorium on AI data centers and battery storage), Hays County (180-day water pause, plus San Marcos's citywide ban), Denton, Cameron (Brownsville and Harlingen moratoria) and Lubbock (moratorium process after a rejected 936-acre rezoning). Other standouts are Somervell, Caldwell, Henderson and Archer, where commissioners opposed or suspended incentives or a developer withdrew, and Hill County, whose moratorium was rescinded after a $100 million lawsuit. Many other high-scoring counties rest mainly on a county-wide base rate with thin local evidence, and several (Armstrong, Young, Nolan) lean toward approval because their commissioners or councils have backed projects, while Texas counties' limited zoning authority tempers what resistance can achieve."
  },
